description:
Replace equestrian's simple checkpoint helper with the @pm990320/playwright-checkpoint package.

**Current state:**
- `app/e2e/helpers.ts` — single function:
  ```typescript
  export async function checkpoint(page: Page, testInfo: TestInfo, name: string) {
    const screenshot = await page.screenshot({ fullPage: true });
    await testInfo.attach(name, { body: screenshot, contentType: "image/png" });
  }
  ```
- Used in tests as: `await checkpoint(page, testInfo, "01-landing-french")`

**Migration steps:**
1. Install `@pm990320/playwright-checkpoint`
2. Create `app/e2e/fixtures.ts` that extends package's test with checkpoint fixture
3. Update all test files to:
   - Import `test` from local fixtures instead of `@playwright/test`
   - Use fixture-based `checkpoint("name")` instead of `checkpoint(page, testInfo, "name")`
4. Delete the `checkpoint` function from `app/e2e/helpers.ts` (or delete file if that's all it contains)
5. Optionally add globalTeardown for HTML report generation
6. Run full test suite — verify all projects pass

**API change in tests:**
```typescript
// Before:
import { test } from "@playwright/test";
import { checkpoint } from "./helpers";
test("loads", async ({ page }, testInfo) => {
  await checkpoint(page, testInfo, "01-landing");
});

// After:
import { test } from "./fixtures";
test("loads", async ({ page, checkpoint }) => {
  await checkpoint("01-landing");
});
```

**Bonus:** Equestrian immediately gains axe, web vitals, console tracking, and HTML report for free.

**Repo:** /Users/patrick/git/toptal/mira/equestrian
**Playwright config:** /Users/patrick/git/toptal/mira/equestrian/playwright.config.ts
**E2E dir:** /Users/patrick/git/toptal/mira/equestrian/app/e2e/

Extract the core checkpoint capture logic into a standalone function that works with any Playwright Page — no test framework required. This enables MCP integration and any other non-test use case.

## Motivation

Currently, the checkpoint logic is tightly coupled to Playwright's test fixture system (`testInfo`, `test.extend()`, etc.). But the actual work — running collectors against a Page, writing artifacts, building manifests — doesn't inherently need the test framework. Extracting this enables:
1. MCP server integration (browser automation agents)
2. Standalone scripts (CI pipelines, monitoring)
3. Any Node.js code that has a Playwright Page object

## New entrypoint

`playwright-checkpoint/core` — the standalone API, no `@playwright/test` dependency at all.

```typescript
import { captureCheckpoint, createCheckpointSession } from 'playwright-checkpoint/core';
```

## API Design

### Quick one-shot capture

```typescript
import { captureCheckpoint } from 'playwright-checkpoint/core';
import { chromium } from 'playwright';

const browser = await chromium.launch();
const page = await browser.newPage();
await page.goto('https://example.com');

const result = await captureCheckpoint(page, 'homepage', {
  outputDir: './checkpoints',
  collectors: { screenshot: true, axe: true, metadata: true },
});

// result: CheckpointRecord with all artifacts, collector data, summary
console.log(result.collectors.axe?.summary); // { violations: 3 }
console.log(result.collectors.metadata?.summary); // { url, title, lang }
```

### Session-based capture (multiple checkpoints, manifest)

```typescript
import { createCheckpointSession } from 'playwright-checkpoint/core';

const session = await createCheckpointSession(page, {
  outputDir: './checkpoints/login-flow',
  collectors: { screenshot: true, axe: true, webVitals: true, console: true },
  sessionMetadata: {
    environment: 'staging',
    project: 'desktop-chrome',
    title: 'Login Flow',
    tags: ['@user-journey'],
  },
});

// Session handles collector lifecycle (setup/teardown)
await page.goto('/login');
await session.checkpoint('login-page', {
  description: 'The login form',
  highlightSelector: '[data-testid="login-form"]',
  step: 1,
});

await page.fill('#email', 'test@example.com');
await page.click('button[type="submit"]');
await session.checkpoint('after-submit', {
  description: 'Dashboard after login',
  step: 2,
});

// Finalize: runs collector teardowns, writes manifest
const manifest = await session.finalize();
// manifest: CheckpointManifest with all checkpoints
```

## Implementation

### File: `src/core.ts`

Refactor the internals from `src/fixture.ts`:

1. **`captureCheckpoint(page, name, options)`** — one-shot function
   - Creates temp output dir if not specified
   - Resolves collectors from options
   - Runs collector pipeline (setup → collect → teardown for each)
   - Returns `CheckpointRecord`
   - No `testInfo` dependency anywhere

2. **`createCheckpointSession(page, options)`** — stateful session
   - Manages collector lifecycle across multiple checkpoints
   - Calls `setup()` on all enabled collectors at creation
   - Each `checkpoint()` call runs `collect()` on all enabled collectors
   - `finalize()` calls `teardown()` on all collectors, writes manifest
   - Incremental tracking for console/network collectors works across checkpoints

3. **Shared internals** — both `captureCheckpoint` and `createCheckpointSession` use the same:
   - `resolveCollectors()` — config resolution
   - `runCollectorPipeline()` — execute collectors, gather results
   - `sanitizeSegment()` — slug generation
   - `settlePage()` — page settling logic
   - Built-in collector registry

### Refactor `src/fixture.ts`

The test fixture becomes a thin wrapper:

```typescript
// src/fixture.ts (simplified after refactor)
import { createCheckpointSession } from './core.js';

// The fixture just bridges testInfo → session
checkpoint: async ({ page, testCheckpointConfig }, use, testInfo) => {
  const session = await createCheckpointSession(page, {
    outputDir: testInfo.outputPath('checkpoints'),
    collectors: mergeConfig(globalConfig, testCheckpointConfig),
    sessionMetadata: {
      environment: process.env.NODE_ENV ?? 'test',
      project: testInfo.project.name,
      title: testInfo.title,
      tags: collectTags(testInfo),
      testId: testInfo.testId,
    },
  });

  const checkpointFn = (name, opts) => session.checkpoint(name, opts);
  await use(checkpointFn);

  // On teardown, finalize and attach manifest
  const manifest = await session.finalize();
  await testInfo.attach('checkpoint-manifest', {
    body: JSON.stringify(manifest, null, 2),
    contentType: 'application/json',
  });
},
```

### What moves where

| Current location | Moves to | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Collector pipeline execution | `src/core.ts` | No testInfo refs |
| `resolveCollectors()` | `src/core.ts` | Already pure |
| `sanitizeSegment()` | `src/core.ts` or `src/utils.ts` | Already pure |
| `settlePage()` | `src/page-utils.ts` | Already there |
| `collectTags()`, `titleParts()` | Stay in `src/fixture.ts` | Test-framework specific |
| Manifest writing | `src/core.ts` via `session.finalize()` | Now standalone |
| `testInfo.attach()` calls | Stay in `src/fixture.ts` | Test-framework specific |
| `testInfo.setTimeout()` (axe budget) | Collector gets optional `adjustTimeout` callback | Graceful no-op outside tests |

### Collector interface update

The `CollectorContext` needs to work without `testInfo`:

```typescript
type CollectorContext = {
  page: Page;                       // Required
  testInfo?: TestInfo;              // Optional — only present in test fixture
  checkpointDir: string;            // Required — where to write files
  checkpointName: string;
  checkpointSlug: string;
  config: ResolvedCollectorConfig;
  options: CheckpointOptions;
  adjustTimeout?: (ms: number) => void;  // Optional callback (maps to testInfo.setTimeout in tests)
};
```

Collectors that currently use `testInfo` (mainly axe for timeout budget) should use `adjustTimeout` callback instead.

### Build config update

Add `src/core.ts` as a tsup entry point:

```typescript
// tsup.config.ts
entry: {
  index: 'src/index.ts',
  core: 'src/core.ts',        // NEW
  teardown: 'src/teardown.ts',
  'cli/index': 'src/cli/index.ts',
  'cli/bin': 'src/cli/bin.ts',
  components: 'src/components.ts',
}
```

Add to package.json exports:
```json
"./core": {
  "import": "./dist/core.js",
  "require": "./dist/core.cjs",
  "types": "./dist/core.d.ts"
}
```

### Key constraint: no @playwright/test dependency in core

`src/core.ts` must NOT import from `@playwright/test`. It should only use `import type { Page }` from `playwright-core` (or accept `page: any` and duck-type). This means the `/core` entrypoint can be used with bare `playwright` (not just `@playwright/test`).

### Tests

- Test `captureCheckpoint()` with a mock page (verify collector pipeline runs, artifacts written, record returned)
- Test `createCheckpointSession()` lifecycle (setup → multiple checkpoints → finalize → manifest written)
- Test that the refactored `fixture.ts` still passes all existing fixture tests
- Test that `/core` entrypoint doesn't transitively import `@playwright/test`

### After you're done
```bash
bd close playwright-checkpoint-<id> --reason "Standalone captureCheckpoint() extracted"
```

Implement an MCP server that proxies any Playwright MCP (official or community) and injects checkpoint tools. Users replace their existing Playwright MCP config with ours — they get all standard tools plus structured page analysis.

## User experience

### Install
```bash
npm install playwright-checkpoint @playwright/mcp @modelcontextprotocol/sdk
```

### Config (replaces their existing @playwright/mcp entry)
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "playwright": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["playwright-checkpoint", "mcp"]
    }
  }
}
```

All existing Playwright tools work unchanged. Three new tools appear:
- `browser_checkpoint` — structured page snapshot with configurable collectors
- `browser_checkpoint_report` — generate report from session checkpoints
- `browser_checkpoint_compare` — diff two checkpoints (later, can be stub initially)

### CLI
```bash
# Start MCP proxy (default: auto-detect upstream)
npx playwright-checkpoint mcp

# Explicit upstream
npx playwright-checkpoint mcp --upstream @playwright/mcp
npx playwright-checkpoint mcp --upstream playwright-mcp-advanced

# Pass-through args to upstream
npx playwright-checkpoint mcp -- --headless --browser chrome

# Standalone (no upstream, just checkpoint tools, user provides CDP endpoint)
npx playwright-checkpoint mcp --standalone --cdp-endpoint http://localhost:9222
```

